     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 5,100 mg/day of metformin is twice the maximum labeled daily dose. A 1700 mg single dose is also well above the usual maximum. No exception I know of applies to a 70 kg adult with normal renal and hepatic function. The risks are serious: lactic acidosis, severe GI toxicity, and possible hospitalization or death.\n\nI can enter a dose within the labeled range right away:\n- **850 mg PO three times daily with meals** (2,550 mg/day, the immediate-release maximum)\n- **1000 mg PO twice daily with meals**\n- **Any other dose and frequency within labeled limits**\n\nIf you have a clinical reason to go above the maximum, such as a protocol, a research setting, or a documented specialist recommendation, I'd suggest placing the order directly in the EHR. That route would involve pharmacy review and the dose-range override process, so a second clinician can check it. I can't bypass that safeguard."
      }
     ],
